// Module 8 quiz - Deep Learning for AI Startups
$CATEGORY: $course$/Module 8

::M8 Q1:: What should you try before fine-tuning? {
~Training from scratch
=Prompting, possibly with examples#Prompting is the cheapest and fastest thing to try. Fine-tune only if it cannot reach the quality, format, speed or cost you need.
~Buying more GPUs
~Deleting the dataset
}

::M8 Q2:: Fine-tuning is best suited to: {
~Adding large amounts of frequently changing facts
~Replacing evaluation
~Reducing the need for data
=Consistent format, tone, or a narrow task#Fine-tuning teaches habits and formats. Facts that change belong in retrieval (RAG), not in the weights.
}

::M8 Q3:: What does LoRA do? {
~Compresses images
~Removes the tokenizer
=Trains small adapter matrices instead of all weights#The original weights stay frozen and only small added matrices are trained, so training is cheap and the saved adapter is tiny.
~Labels data
}

::M8 Q4:: Which three axes should you compare when choosing prompting or fine-tuning? {
=Quality, cost, latency#A good choice balances how good the answers are, what each request costs, and how long users wait.
~Color, size, shape
~Epochs, layers, seeds
~Name, license, date
}

::M8 Q5:: For a task that needs up-to-date company facts, which is usually better? {
~Fine-tuning on a snapshot only
=RAG#Facts change, and retrieval looks them up fresh each time. A fine-tuned model would need retraining whenever the facts change.
~Training from scratch
~No retrieval
}
